## Introduction to Deletion in Doubly Linked List
A Doubly Linked List (DLL) is a linear data structure where each node contains three components: data, a pointer to the next node, and a pointer to the previous node. This bidirectional linking makes deletion operations more flexible and efficient compared to singly linked lists.

## Node Structure Definition
The basic node structure that forms the building block of our doubly linked list is defined as:
```java
class Node {
    int data;       // Data stored in the node
    Node next;      // Reference to next node
    Node prev;      // Reference to previous node

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
        this.prev = null;
    }
}
```

## Three Cases of Deletion in DLL
The three cases of deletion are categorized based on the position of the node to be removed from the list:

*   **Case 1: Delete First Node (Head)**
    *   Time Complexity: O(1)
    *   Key Operations: Update head pointer
*   **Case 2: Delete Last Node (Tail)**
    *   Time Complexity: O(1)
    *   Key Operations: Update tail pointer
*   **Case 3: Delete Middle Node**
    *   Time Complexity: O(n) to find, O(1) to delete
    *   Key Operations: Update both neighbors

### Case 1: Delete the First Node (Head)
Deleting the first node (head) is one of the most common operations in a doubly linked list. This operation requires updating the head pointer to point to the second node, and setting the new head's previous pointer to null.

#### Implementation Code
```java
public void deleteFirst() {
    // Case: Empty list
    if (head == null) {
        System.out.println("List is empty, nothing to delete.");
        return;
    }

    // Case: Single node in list
    if (head.next == null) {
        head = null;
        tail = null;
    } else {
        // Case: Multiple nodes - delete first
        head = head.next;      // Move head to second node
        head.prev = null;      // Set new head's prev to null
    }

    size--;  // Decrement size
}
```

### Case 2: Delete the Last Node (Tail)
Deleting the last node (tail) demonstrates one of the key advantages of a doubly linked list over a singly linked list. In a singly linked list, deleting the last node requires O(n) time to traverse and find the second-to-last node. However, in a DLL, we can access the previous node directly through the prev pointer, making this operation O(1).

#### Implementation Code
```java
public void deleteLast() {
    // Case: Empty list
    if (head == null) {
        System.out.println("List is empty, nothing to delete.");
        return;
    }

    // Case: Single node in list
    if (head.next == null) {
        head = null;
        tail = null;
    } else {
        // Case: Multiple nodes - delete last
        tail = tail.prev;      // Move tail to second-to-last node
        tail.next = null;      // Set new tail's next to null
    }

    size--;  // Decrement size
}
```

### Case 3: Delete a Node from the Middle
Deleting a node from the middle of the list is the most complex case among the three. This operation requires updating both neighboring nodes to bypass the deleted node. While finding the node to delete takes O(n) time, the actual deletion operation after finding the node is O(1), which is another advantage of DLL over SLL.

#### Implementation Code
```java
public void deleteAtIndex(int index) {
    // Case: Invalid index
    if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
        throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Invalid index");
    }

    // Case: Delete first node (redirects to Case 1)
    if (index == 0) {
        deleteFirst();
        return;
    }

    // Case: Delete last node (redirects to Case 2)
    if (index == size - 1) {
        deleteLast();
        return;
    }

    // Case: Delete middle node
    Node current = head;
    for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
        current = current.next;
    }

    // Link the previous node to the next node
    current.prev.next = current.next;

    // Link the next node to the previous node
    current.next.prev = current.prev;

    size--;  // Decrement size
}
```

