## Summary
The following table summarizes the key differences between the three deletion cases in a Doubly Linked List:

| Case | Time Complexity | Pointer Updates | Special Handling |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Delete First | O(1) | head.prev = null | Single node case |
| Delete Last | O(1) | tail.next = null | Single node case |
| Delete Middle | O(n) find + O(1) delete | Both neighbors | Invalid index check |

In conclusion, the Doubly Linked List provides efficient deletion operations at both ends of the list in O(1) time, which is a significant advantage over Singly Linked Lists. The presence of both prev and next pointers enables bidirectional traversal and simplifies the deletion process by eliminating the need to traverse the list to find the previous node.

